# terraform

Deploys {{ project_name }} to AWS Lambda via the shared
[`lambda-web-app`](https://github.com/NateDogg12501/terraform-modules/tree/main/modules/lambda-web-app)
module{% if needs_datastore %},
[`dynamodb-single-table`](https://github.com/NateDogg12501/terraform-modules/tree/main/modules/dynamodb-single-table){% endif %},
with state in the S3 bucket shared across every project generated from this
template — not a bucket this project owns.

Every `source =` in here is a tag-pinned `terraform-modules` reference, and the
`terraform` CI job fails if one isn't — add resources to that repo and release a
tag, not to this directory. See
[STANDARDS.md](https://github.com/NateDogg12501/project-template/blob/main/STANDARDS.md)'s
Hosting section.

## Environments

Every apply targets one environment — `staging` or `production` today —
supplied both at `init` (which state key) and at `apply`/`plan` (which
resource names). `var.environment` (see `variables.tf`) is validated against
that same list and has no default: every apply says explicitly which
environment it's targeting.

## The normal path is the deploy workflow, not this directory

`gh workflow run deploy.yml --field environment=staging` runs everything below
and rather more: the account-wide DynamoDB capacity gate, creating this
environment's session secret if it is missing, building the Lambda artifact,
`plan` to a file and `apply` of that file,{% if needs_datastore %} reseeding staging from
sanitized production data,{% endif %} and a health check on the deployed URL. See
[`../docs/deploy.md`](../docs/deploy.md).

What follows is the by-hand path — useful for a first look or a `destroy`, and
the thing the workflow's `terraform-apply` stage automates. Applying by hand
skips the capacity gate and the secret creation, so read step 2.

## Before first `terraform apply`

1. Build the Lambda artifact:
   ```bash
   cd backend && npm run build:lambda
   ```
   This runs `backend/scripts/build-lambda.js`, which stages `backend/src`,
   production-only `node_modules`,{% if needs_ui %} `frontend/`'s static files (into
   `dist-lambda/web`, matching the `WEB_DIST` that `main.tf` sets),{% endif %} and the
   `run.sh` the Lambda Web Adapter execs. Re-run it after **any** {% if needs_ui %}backend or
   frontend{% else %}backend{% endif %} change — `terraform apply` zips this directory, it does not
   build it.
2. Create every SSM parameter referenced in `main.tf`'s `ssm_secret_env_vars`:
   ```bash
   aws ssm put-parameter --name "/{{ project_slug }}/<environment>/..." --type SecureString --value "..."
   ```
   The deploy workflow's `ensure-secrets` stage does this for
   `session_secret` — create-if-absent, never rotating, since regenerating it
   would log every user out on every deploy. A by-hand apply has not run that
   stage, so an uncommented `ssm_secret_env_vars` entry fails the plan until
   the parameter exists.
3. Init against the environment you're deploying, then apply:
   ```bash
   cd terraform
   terraform init -backend-config="key={{ project_slug }}/staging/terraform.tfstate"
   terraform apply -var="environment=staging"
   ```
   Swap `staging` for `production` to target the other environment — same
   config, different backend key and `-var`. Switching environments in an
   already-initialized directory needs `terraform init -reconfigure
   -backend-config="key=..."` — plain `init` reuses the backend config already
   on disk.

`terraform apply` needs `python3` on PATH — the module zips the artifact with
a helper script rather than `archive_file`, so `run.sh` keeps its executable
bit even when the zip is built on Windows.

To check syntax without AWS access, `terraform init -backend=false && terraform
validate` — a plain `init` now contacts S3, and fails without credentials.
CI always runs this. Once this project has been through per-project AWS
provisioning, CI also plans against both environments' real state on every
pull request — see `.github/workflows/ci.yml`'s `terraform` job.

## Rotating a secret takes two steps

`lambda-web-app` reads every `ssm_secret_env_vars` parameter at **apply** time
and bakes the value into the Lambda's environment. **There is no runtime
lookup.** So:

> `aws ssm put-parameter` changes SSM and changes nothing that is deployed.
> This app keeps serving the value it was applied with until it is deployed
> again.

Nothing errors and nothing warns in between, which is what makes it worth a
section. The symptom is a credential that is correct in SSM and rejected by the
running app — and, for a parameter shared with other projects, correct in one
app and rejected in another at the same time.

So rotating a secret is:

1. `aws ssm put-parameter --overwrite` with the new value.
2. Redeploy **every environment** that consumes it — not just the one you were
   looking at. `gh workflow run deploy.yml --field environment=staging`, then
   the same for `production`.

To check whether what is deployed matches SSM, without printing either:

```bash
aws lambda get-function-configuration --function-name {{ project_slug }}-staging --query 'Environment.Variables.SESSION_SECRET' --output text > /tmp/a
aws ssm get-parameter --name /{{ project_slug }}/staging/session_secret --with-decryption --query 'Parameter.Value' --output text > /tmp/b
cmp -s /tmp/a /tmp/b && echo current || echo STALE; rm -f /tmp/a /tmp/b
```

Account-wide, across every project and environment at once, `aws-account` has
`scripts/credential-doctor.js`, which does the same comparison by SSM version
number and writes nothing to disk.

**`/shared/auth_username` and `/shared/auth_password_hash` are worse than they
look**, because they are shared with every other project on the account.
Rotating one leaves every *other* project serving the old value until each of
them redeploys too, so the account runs a mix until they all catch up — a
correct password accepted by some apps and refused by others. `aws-account`'s
`scripts/rotate-shared-credential.js` does that propagation and verifies it.

`session_secret` is per-project-per-environment, so its blast radius is this
project alone — but the mechanism is identical, and rotating it logs everyone
out of that environment, which is usually the point.

## State

State for each environment is at
`{{ project_slug }}/<environment>/terraform.tfstate` in the shared bucket,
supplied as `backend.tf`'s `key` at `init` — backend blocks can't interpolate,
so it can't be templated in directly. Locking is `use_lockfile = true` —
S3-native, per key, so there's no DynamoDB lock table and no contention
between environments or other projects sharing the bucket.

Isolation from other projects in the same bucket is IAM, not bucket
topology: this project's deploy role is scoped to `s3:*Object` on
`arn:aws:s3:::<bucket>/{{ project_slug }}/*` and nothing wider. The bucket
itself — creation, versioning, lifecycle policy — is managed outside this
project, in the account-level bootstrap; there is nothing here to create,
lose, or re-apply.

## Tearing it down

`terraform destroy -var="environment=<environment>"`, per environment. Unlike
a per-project bucket, there's no second config to tear down afterward and no
ordering to get wrong — the bucket outlives every project's `destroy` by
construction.
{% if needs_ui %}

## Adding static assets

Nothing to do — `build-lambda.js` copies all of `frontend/` into
`dist-lambda/web` except a fixed set of tooling files (`node_modules`,
`package.json`, `package-lock.json`, `test/`, `vitest.config.js`). A new asset
ships automatically. The flip side is that anything you leave in `frontend/`
is published, so don't park scratch files or secrets there.
{% endif %}
